// Walking the statement tree. C has fifteen statement forms and `id` allows
// three actions per block, so the choice is made one question at a time down
// a chain of functions -- the same shape the parser uses. Each link asks
// about exactly one node kind; the last link is the default, which appends
// the statement to the block being filled.
//
// The invariant the whole walker rests on: **the current block is never
// terminated**. Anything that ends a block (`return`, `goto`, `break`,
// `continue`) opens a fresh one immediately, so no terminator is ever written
// twice and unreachable code still has somewhere to go -- it may yet contain
// a label that a goto jumps to.

walk_list(int[] ids) {
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len(ids)) {
    walk_stmt(ids[i]);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return void;
given walk_ast_fixture ([]):() then calls walk_stmt:[0]
given walk_ast_fixture ([0]):() then calls walk_stmt:[1]

walk_stmt(int id) {
  if(k_of(id) == "block") {
    int[] l = l_of(id);
    walk_list(l);
  } else {
    walk_stmt2(id);
  }
} return void;
given walk_ast_fixture (7):() then calls walk_list:[1]
given walk_ast_fixture (5):() then calls walk_stmt2:[1]

walk_stmt2(int id) {
  if(k_of(id) == "if") {
    do_if(id);
  } else {
    walk_stmt3(id);
  }
} return void;
